= Ambiguity — Technical =

== Representational ambiguity ==
An expression is ambiguous when the grammar/lexicon licenses distinct interpretations that are not merely differences in contextual precision.

Common types include lexical ambiguity, syntactic/attachment ambiguity, quantifier or operator scope ambiguity, referential ambiguity, and semantic type/coercion ambiguity.

== Structural derivation ==
If one surface string s admits distinct parses p₁ and p₂:

<pre>
parse(s) → {p₁, p₂}
</pre>

and semantic interpretation maps them to distinct contents:

<pre>
⟦p₁⟧ ≠ ⟦p₂⟧
</pre>

the ambiguity can be represented structurally.

Other frameworks use underspecification rather than fully enumerating readings.

== Lexical representation ==
Lexical ambiguity can be represented as multiple lexemes/senses, a structured polysemous entry, generative lexical rules, probabilistic contextual representations, or latent contextualized vectors.

These choices encode theoretical commitments.

== Diagnostics ==
Common diagnostics include zeugma, coordination, ellipsis, contradiction tests, contextual compatibility, and distributional behavior. No diagnostic is universally decisive.

== Computational disambiguation ==
Word-sense disambiguation and semantic parsing can be framed as selecting or inferring an interpretation given context.

Accuracy depends on the sense inventory and task definition; a benchmark label set is not a theory-neutral inventory of meanings.

